      "findings": [
        {
          "source_quote": "ἑκάστου τῆς ἐκκλησίας πολυπραγμονοῦντες τὰς πράξεις τίς μέλλει ἁμαρτάνειν",
          "english_quote": "closely examining the conduct of each person in the church—who is about to sin—",
          "issue": "τίς introduces the inquiry into who is about to sin. The English dashes make the phrase liable to be read as a relative description of each person instead. The lexical note recognizes the interrogative, but the main wording does not clearly convey it.",
          "suggested_revision": "Use “closely examining the conduct of each person in the church to see who is about to sin.” This proposed wording requires fresh review.",
          "severity": "minor"
        },
        {
          "source_quote": "τοῦ μὴ ἐπιλείπειν τοὺς συνερχομένους τῶν λόγων ἐπακούειν",
          "english_quote": "so that those who assemble do not fail to listen to the discourses",
          "issue": "This remains a possible interpretation, not an established analysis. The accusative τοὺς συνερχομένους can be analyzed as the subject or object of ἐπιλείπειν, and ἐπακούειν can attach to ἐπιλείπειν or συνερχομένους. The cited Antiphon example establishes an infinitival construction but, with its dative pronoun and negative infinitive, does not settle these particular attachments. The existing source_issues disclosure is appropriate, but its publication-blocking meaning question remains unresolved.",
          "suggested_revision": "Keep the rendering explicitly provisional. Compare the three analyses against closer syntactic parallels, preferably from this author: assembled people failing to listen; people assembled to listen falling away; and the deacons not abandoning those assembled to listen. Retain the alternatives in source_issues and do not treat the construction as corrupt or any replacement as already accepted.",
          "severity": "major"
        },
        {
          "source_quote": "καὶ τῷ ἀγνοοῦντι πλήθει προσαντιβαλλέτωσαν, ἵν’ ἐπιφαίνωνται",
          "english_quote": "and bring them to the attention of the multitude that is unaware, so that they may present themselves",
          "issue": "The rendering is plausible, but the subject of ἐπιφαίνωνται is not established. The sick people and members of the collectively expressed multitude remain possible referents. Evidence that passive ἐπιφαίνω can mean appearing or presenting oneself does not identify who appears here or establish a visit to the sick. The note correctly discloses this uncertainty; it does not resolve the meaning question that the draft marks as publication-blocking.",
          "suggested_revision": "Retain the selected Greek and the unresolved subject in source_issues. Seek contextual or author-specific parallels before accepting a subject-specific rendering. “May appear” or “may present themselves” can remain provisional; “visit them” would require an explicit statement that the visitation and its object are contextual inferences.",
          "severity": "major"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "reference": "15",
      "verdict": "revise",
      "checks": {
        "complete_coverage": true,
        "no_added_claims": true,
        "names_and_negations": true,
        "lexical_fidelity": false,
        "uncertainties_disclosed": true
      },
      "findings": [
        {
          "source_quote": "ὁ ἐπίσκοπος ὡς πρωρεὺς ’ἐγρηγορὼς τοῦ κυβερνήτου μόνου τοὺς λόγους ἀντιβαλλέτω",
          "english_quote": "The bishop, as a bow officer keeping watch, should respond to the words of the helmsman alone.",
          "issue": "“Respond to” has some support from the conversational sense of ἀντιβάλλω, but the cited reciprocal exchange construction does not establish this particular action. Here τοὺς λόγους is the direct object, and τοῦ κυβερνήτου μόνου identifies whose words they are; no interlocutor or onward recipient is expressed. Responding, exchanging, repeating, and relaying therefore cannot be treated as interchangeable established meanings. The draft appropriately records alternatives, but its explicitly publication-blocking lexical question remains open.",
          "suggested_revision": "Keep “respond to” provisional rather than certifying it as the settled sense. Compare author-specific uses and relevant nautical command language before choosing among responding, exchanging, or repeating the helmsman’s words. Onward transmission in “relay” remains an additional contextual inference. Preserve the uncertainty in source_issues; this is a meaning problem, not demonstrated corruption or a reason to alter the printed mark before ἐγρηγορὼς. Any selected replacement needs fresh review.",
          "severity": "major"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
